questions.question:
A. Describe oxytetracycline under the following headings:
(a) source and composition;
(b) mechanism of action and spectrum;
(c) pharmacokinetics;
(d) indications, doses and dosage forms.
B. State its adverse effects and related precautions:
(e) toxicity in fish and invertebrates;
(f) human health concerns;
(g) contraindications and prescribing precautions;
C. Explain the following implications:
(h) residues and environmental persistence;
(i) resistance mechanisms;
(j) mitigation and regulation.

A. Describe the benzimidazoles within the endoparasiticide drug groups under the following headings:
(a) classification of endoparasiticide groups;
(b) mechanism of action of benzimidazoles;
(c) spectrum and uses;
(d) dosage forms and prodrugs.
B. Explain the pharmacological basis of their advantages and limitations:
(e) properties of carbamate derivatives;
(f) residue distribution;
(g) contraindication;
(h) ideal characteristics of a parasiticide.
C. Distinguish between:
(i) benzimidazoles and macrocyclic lactones;
(j) levamisole and benzimidazoles;
(k) pyrantel and morantel;
(l) avermectins and benzimidazoles in aquaculture.

A. Describe diazinon under the following headings:
(a) classification and chemical nature;
(b) mechanism of action;
(c) metabolism and pharmacokinetics;
(d) indications, species and dosage forms.
B. State its adverse effects and related precautions:
(e) toxicity in animals and humans;
(f) aquatic toxicity;
(g) contraindications and precautions;
C. Explain the following implications:
(h) environmental persistence;
(i) residues in water and the food chain;
(j) resistance and regulation.

A. Describe veterinary drug regulation for food-producing animals:
(a) the meaning of withdrawal period;
(b) the meaning and importance of the maximum residue limit (MRL);
(c) why strict regulation is required;
(d) the agencies and instruments named.
B. Relate these principles to the following examples:
(e) tylosin;
(f) oxytetracycline;
(g) olaquindox;
(h) growth-promoter restrictions and bans.
C. Explain why the principles matter and what limitation or trade-off results:
(i) animal safety and efficacy;
(j) public health;
(k) the limitation of reform;
(l) the trade-off seen after bans.

A. Describe veterinary pharmacognosy:
(a) the definition;
(b) the history of plant use;
(c) the examples of self-medication by animals;
(d) the therapeutic categories used.
B. Relate its parts to common examples of plants and products:
(e) analgesic and anti-inflammatory plants;
(f) antiparasitic and antibacterial plants;
(g) digestive and reproductive plants;
(h) cardiovascular and anti-tumour plants and proprietary products.
C. Explain why it matters and what limitation or trade-off results:
(i) animal safety and toxic plants;
(j) efficacy and preparation;
(k) public health and legal status;
(l) dosing limitations and product claims.

A. Describe antiseptics and disinfectants used in animals under the following headings:
(a) classification and nature;
(b) agents for teats and udder;
(c) agents for general first aid;
(d) dosage forms and mechanism of action.
B. Explain the pharmacological basis of the properties required of an ideal product:
(e) spectrum, onset and duration;
(f) stability and activity in organic material;
(g) lipid solubility and dispersibility;
(h) toxicity and healing.
C. Distinguish between:
(i) disinfectants for teats and udder and antiseptics for first aid;
(j) antiseptics and antibiotics in mastitis;
(k) a teat dip and a shampoo containing chlorhexidine;
(l) topical antiseptic wash and systemic therapy.

A. Describe the trypanocidal drugs used in livestock under the following headings:
(a) classification and members;
(b) mechanism of action;
(c) spectrum and uses;
B. Explain the pharmacological basis of their advantages and limitations:
(d) diminazene aceturate;
(e) isometamidium chloride;
(f) suramin;
(g) resistance and therapeutic concentrations.
C. Distinguish between:
(h) diminazene and isometamidium;
(i) suramin and the other two drugs;
(j) synthetic trypanocides and plant remedies;
(k) trypanosomiasis and the other protozoal infections tabulated.

A. Describe pesticides as a veterinary and agricultural principle:
(a) definition and purpose;
(b) the types of pesticide;
(c) the chemical and natural classes;
(d) the classification of insecticides.
B. Relate their parts to examples:
(e) organophosphate examples;
(f) recently banned pesticides;
(g) pyrethrins, pyrethroids, derris and tobacco;
(h) rodenticides and molluscicides.
C. Explain why they matter and what limitation or trade-off results:
(i) benefits for animal and crop protection;
(j) risks to health and the environment;
(k) requirements for new pesticides;
(l) the trade-off in natural products.

A. Describe veterinary epidemiology for pharmacists:
(a) the definition;
(b) the objectives;
(c) the four types of investigation;
(d) the subdisciplines.
B. Relate its parts to examples of disease investigation:
(e) foot-and-mouth disease;
(f) bovine spongiform encephalopathy and contagious bovine pleuropneumonia;
(g) bovine tuberculosis and malignant catarrhal fever;
(h) tsetse fly, Fasciola hepatica and leptospirosis.
C. Explain why it matters and what limitation or trade-off results:
(i) surveys, screening, monitoring and surveillance;
(j) the types of study;
(k) the economic value of control programmes;
(l) limitations and trade-offs of study design and risk assessment.

A. Describe the organophosphate and carbamate ectoparasiticides under the following headings:
(a) classification and examples;
(b) mechanism of action;
(c) spectrum and uses;
(d) dosage forms.
B. Explain the pharmacological basis of their advantages and limitations in animals:
(e) mode of poisoning and spectrum of action;
(f) bioactivation to a more potent metabolite;
(g) resistance;
(h) toxicity and human safety.
C. Distinguish between:
(i) organophosphates and formamidines;
(j) organophosphates and nitroguanidines;
(k) organophosphates and phenylpyrazoles;
(l) diazinon and chlorfenvinphos.

A. Describe antibiotic growth promoters used in animal feeds under the following headings:
(a) definition and examples by species;
(b) mechanisms of action;
(c) benefits and uses;
(d) dosage forms and routes.
B. Explain the pharmacological basis of their advantages and limitations:
(e) energy salvage in the gut;
(f) greater effect under poor conditions;
(g) selection of resistance in zoonotic bacteria;
(h) effect on sanitation.
C. Distinguish between:
(i) monensin and virginiamycin;
(j) avoparcin and vancomycin;
(k) therapeutic and growth-promoting doses;
(l) practice in the USA, the EU and Sweden.

A. Describe flumethrin under the following headings:
(a) source and composition;
(b) physicochemical properties;
(c) pharmacokinetics and metabolism;
(d) indications, species and dosage forms.
B. State its adverse effects and related precautions:
(e) adverse effects in animals;
(f) contraindications and species differences;
(g) effects in humans and handling precautions;
C. Explain the following implications:
(h) environmental residue and toxicity;
(i) resistance;
(j) reporting and monitoring.

A. Describe ivermectin under the following headings:
(a) source and chemical nature;
(b) mechanism of action;
(c) indications and species;
(d) doses and dosage forms.
B. State its metabolism and adverse effects:
(e) metabolism and the enzymes involved;
(f) role of transport proteins;
(g) toxicity, safety margin and contraindications;
(h) drug interactions.
C. Explain the following implications:
(i) residue and environmental persistence;
(j) resistance;
(k) licensing and regulatory status.

A. Describe the One Health concept:
(a) the definition;
(b) the link between human, animal and ecosystem health and the figures given;
(c) the stressors that favour disease emergence;
(d) the range of issues covered.
B. Relate its parts to the common examples given:
(e) zoonotic diseases;
(f) vector-borne diseases;
(g) food safety and foodborne diseases;
(h) environmental health and antimicrobial resistance.
C. Explain why it matters and what limitation or trade-off results:
(i) animal and environmental safety;
(j) public health;
(k) the trade-off or limitation.

A. Describe tylosin under the following headings:
(a) source and composition;
(b) properties, mechanism and spectrum;
(c) pharmacokinetics;
(d) indications, species and doses.
B. State its adverse effects and related precautions:
(e) adverse effects;
(f) acute toxicity;
(g) contraindications and precautions;
C. Explain the following implications:
(h) residues;
(i) resistance mechanism;
(j) mitigation and regulatory restriction.

A. Describe botanical insecticides under the following headings:
(a) classification and sources;
(b) mechanism of action;
(c) spectrum and veterinary uses;
(d) dosage forms and preparation.
B. Explain the pharmacological basis of their advantages and limitations:
(e) pyrethrins;
(f) rotenone-containing plants;
(g) tobacco nicotinoids;
(h) azadirachtin.
C. Distinguish between:
(i) pyrethrins and pyrethroids;
(j) rotenone and nicotine;
(k) neem and derris;
(l) botanical insecticides and organophosphates.

A. Describe the sulfonamides used in animals under the following headings:
(a) chemical nature and discovery;
(b) classification with examples;
(c) mechanism of action;
(d) spectrum, veterinary uses and dosage forms.
B. Explain the pharmacological basis of their advantages and limitations:
(e) selective toxicity;
(f) bacteriostatic action and anaerobes;
(g) metabolism and species differences;
(h) resistance and adverse effects.
C. Distinguish between:
(i) sulfamethazine and sulfadiazine;
(j) antibiotic and non-antibiotic sulfonamides;
(k) a sulfonamide alone and a sulfonamide with trimethoprim;
(l) the pig metabolites of sulfamethazine and their half-lives.

A. Describe chlorfenvinphos under the following headings:
(a) source and composition;
(b) physicochemical properties;
(c) metabolism and species differences;
(d) indications, species, doses and application.
B. State its adverse effects and related precautions:
(e) toxicity and effects on cholinesterase;
(f) toxicity to aquatic organisms;
(g) contraindications and precautions;
C. Explain the following implications:
(h) residues and human exposure;
(i) resistance;
(j) regulation and alternatives.

A. Describe the environmental risk assessment of veterinary drugs:
(a) the routes by which drugs enter the environment;
(b) the occurrence figures given;
(c) the aim and risk indicators of environmental risk assessment (ERA);
(d) the limitations of ERA guidelines and the future directions.
B. Relate its parts to the fate of named drugs:
(e) ivermectin;
(f) tylosin;
(g) sulfonamides;
(h) olaquindox, flumethrin and chlorfenvinphos.
C. Explain why it matters and what limitation or trade-off results:
(i) safety of non-target organisms;
(j) public health;
(k) the limitation of current guidance;
(l) the trade-off between drug value and environmental vigilance.

A. Describe the penicillins used in veterinary practice under the following headings:
(a) classification and members named;
(b) spectrum and uses;
(c) routes and vehicles.
B. Explain the pharmacological basis of their limitations in animals:
(d) chemical instability;
(e) species restrictions;
(f) beta-lactamase resistance;
(g) emergence of resistant strains.
C. Distinguish between:
(h) benzylpenicillin and phenoxymethylpenicillin;
(i) procaine benzylpenicillin and flucloxacillin;
(j) amoxicillin and benzylpenicillin;
(k) penicillins and cephalosporins.

A. Describe ethinyl estradiol (EE2) under the following headings:
(a) source and chemical nature;
(b) properties and mechanism of action;
(c) pharmacokinetics;
(d) indications and species.
B. State its adverse effects and related precautions:
(e) adverse effects and cautions in animals;
(f) endocrine-disrupting toxicity;
C. Explain the following implications:
(g) withdrawal period and growth promotion;
(h) residue and environmental persistence;
(i) public health and mitigation.

A. Describe pyrethrins and pyrethroids as ectoparasiticides under the following headings:
(a) source and classification (natural pyrethrins, first-generation and second-generation synthetic pyrethroids);
(b) mechanism of action;
(c) spectrum and veterinary uses;
(d) dosage forms and routes of administration.
B. Explain the pharmacological basis of their advantages and limitations in animals:
(e) onset and duration of action and the use of synergists;
(f) safety in cats and dogs and the basis of feline sensitivity;
(g) photostability and residual activity;
(h) selectivity for arthropods compared with mammals.
C. Distinguish between:
(i) pyrethrin I and pyrethrin II;
(j) natural pyrethrins and synthetic pyrethroids;
(k) first-generation and second-generation pyrethroids;
(l) permethrin or deltamethrin and flumethrin.

A. Describe the principles of antimicrobial use in animals:
(a) factors governing the choice of an antimicrobial;
(b) species and drug examples;
(c) mechanisms of bacterial resistance shown by oxytetracycline;
(d) stewardship and mitigation measures.
B. Relate these principles to examples of growth promoters and resistance:
(e) definition and effect of antibiotic growth promoters;
(f) chloramphenicol;
(g) avoparcin;
(h) monensin and virginiamycin.
C. Explain why the principles matter and what limitation or trade-off results:
(i) animal safety and efficacy;
(j) public health;
(k) limitation shown by the Swedish ban;
(l) trade-off in cost and mortality.

A. Describe topical formulations and routes of administration in veterinary practice:
(a) dips;
(b) teat dips;
(c) pour-on and spot-on products;
(d) dust bags and aerosol dispensers.
B. Relate their parts to further examples:
(e) flea and tick collars;
(f) ointments and creams;
(g) shampoos;
(h) routes used for antimicrobials and avermectins.
C. Explain why they matter and what limitation or trade-off results:
(i) animal safety and efficacy;
(j) human and public safety;
(k) limitations of use;
(l) trade-off between convenience and environmental or resistance risk.

A. Describe olaquindox under the following headings:
(a) source and composition;
(b) properties and mechanism of action;
(c) pharmacokinetics and species differences;
(d) indications and doses.
B. State its adverse effects and related precautions:
(e) effects on the target animal;
(f) toxicity in aquatic organisms;
(g) contraindications and use restrictions;
C. Explain the following implications:
(h) residues and bioconcentration;
(i) resistance;
(j) regulation and environmental fate.
